<title>USA: USDA-US soy pods heavy, record Nebraska corn yield.</title>
<headline>USDA-US soy pods heavy, record Nebraska corn yield.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mild weather allowed soybean pods to fill out and boost the forecast U.S. soybean crop by three percent, Agrliculture Department analyst Fred Vogel said Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;The crop has been able to come along fairly well,&quot; Vogel told reporters after USDA released its October crop report with a forecast soybean crop of 2.35 billion bushels.</p>
<p>Pods weighed more than earlier expected, he said, when the slow-to-mature crop appeared to have light pod weights.</p>
<p>He also said pod counts were up in Missouri, Arkansas and Nebraska.</p>
<p>USDA forecast a record corn yield in Nebraska, 143 bushels an acre, he said.</p>
<p>Eastern Corn Belt states still lagged behind other states in corn maturity.</p>
<p>&quot;They've gotten better but haven't caught up,&quot; he said. &quot;Those crops have farther to go too.&quot;</p>
<p>So far, Vogel said, frost dates have been average.</p>
</text>
